**16. GREAT LAKES TREATY WATERS AND MARITIME DIMENSION**
    a) The waters of the 1836 cession retain tribal usufructuary and governance interests, affirmed and co-managed under United States v. Michigan and its Consent Decrees (most recently the decree entered August 24, 2023, with a term through August 2047; affirmed by the U.S. Court of Appeals for the Sixth Circuit in March 2025; certiorari denied January 2026), implemented through the Chippewa Ottawa Resource Authority (CORA), of which GTBOCI is a member. Knowledge within the Work concerning fisheries, navigation, ecology, or place-names of the Beaver Island archipelago or other 1836-ceded waters is Treaty-adjacent Traditional Knowledge.
    b) Reserved submerged-lands and water-rights doctrines are preserved without waiver: tribal ownership of the beds of navigable waters where reserved for a tribe (Choctaw Nation v. Oklahoma, 397 U.S. 620 (1970); United States v. Holt State Bank, 270 U.S. 49 (1926); compare Montana v. United States, 450 U.S. 544 (1981)), and federally reserved water rights (Winters v. United States, 207 U.S. 564 (1908)).
    c) Exclusive federal admiralty jurisdiction under 28 U.S.C. § 1333 is neither created nor waived by this license. Users are prohibited from maritime uses of the Work materially interfering with Treaty-Protected Interests (Section 6(p)), and the Rights Holder preserves remedies in admiralty, federal-question (28 U.S.C. § 1331), and tribal forums, without election.
    d) Customary international law is part of United States law (The Paquete Habana, 175 U.S. 677, 700 (1900)). The United States is NOT a party to the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea (UNCLOS), but accepts its navigational and resource-related provisions as customary international law (U.S. Oceans Policy Statement, 1983). This license invokes that framework as interpretive backdrop and as a rule of construction for cross-border users -- not as a claim that UNCLOS binds the United States as a treaty.
    e) Marine treaty-rights precedent informs the defense of reserved rights generally (e.g., the Treaty of Neah Bay (1855), 12 Stat. 939, expressly reserving whaling and sealing rights; the Makah whaling litigation, Anderson v. Evans (9th Cir. 2004)).
    f) The Boundary Waters Treaty of 1909 (36 Stat. 2448) preserves navigation and usage rights in US-Canada boundary waters; users shall not use the Work to undermine Indigenous navigation, fishing, or stewardship rights in boundary and connecting waters of the Great Lakes.

**17. CULTURAL PATRIMONY, BURIAL PROTECTIONS, AND ANTI-ALIENATION**
    17.1 NON-ALIENATION: Cultural Patrimony is inalienable by any individual (NAGPRA, 25 U.S.C. §§ 3001-3002); no transfer or sale can extinguish the Community's interest. No user shall use the Work, its data, or its outputs to facilitate, document for sale, market, broker, or appraise for conveyance any human remains, funerary objects, sacred objects, burial sites (including any portion of the Beaver Island archipelago burial landscape), or objects of Cultural Patrimony.
    17.2 SEPULCHER: The resting places of the ancestors and their associated structures -- including spirit houses erected over burials -- shall not be disturbed, commodified, decorated, "restored," toured, or displayed through or with the Work. The common-law right of sepulcher and the principles of NAGPRA bind all users (25 U.S.C. §§ 3001-3013).
    17.3 DISCOVERY PROTOCOL: Information encountered through the Work indicating the apparent location of human remains, funerary objects, or undocumented sites of cultural significance within the Beaver Island archipelago or other Treaty-ceded territory is restricted Derivative Data: no publication, no commercial use, prompt notice to the Rights Holder (Section 14), and referral to the appropriate tribal authorities.
    17.4 INDIAN ARTS AND CRAFTS ACT: Where material is offered in commerce as authentic Indigenous production, the Indian Arts and Crafts Act applies by its own terms (civil: treble damages or not less than $1,000 per day of violation, 25 U.S.C. § 305e(b); criminal: 18 U.S.C. §§ 1158-1159, enforced by federal authorities, not by this license). Those statutory remedies supplement, and are never supplanted by, this license.

**18. INTERNATIONAL INSTRUMENTS AND THEIR TRUE STATUS**
    This license incorporates the following instruments as interpretive authorities and, where expressly stated, as contractual commitments -- each to the precise extent of its actual legal status for the United States, and never more: UNDRIP (2007; declaration; U.S. endorsement 2010), especially Articles 11, 19, 31, 32, 37, and 40, which are adopted here as contractual commitments of the parties regardless of the Declaration's non-binding status; the American Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples (OAS, adopted June 15, 2016) -- interpretive; the WIPO Treaty on Intellectual Property, Genetic Resources and Associated Traditional Knowledge (adopted May 24, 2024) -- the United States has NOT signed; its Article 3 disclosure principle is adopted contractually (Section 6(q)); ILO Convention No. 169 (1989) -- U.S. non-party, persuasive; ICCPR Articles 1 and 27 -- ratified 1992 with reservations, declared non-self-executing, interpretive; ICESCR Article 15(1)(c) -- signed 1977, NOT ratified, persuasive; ICERD Article 5(e)(v) -- ratified 1994 with reservations, declared non-self-executing, interpretive; the Convention on Biological Diversity, Articles 8(j) and 10(c) -- U.S. non-party, persuasive; the Nagoya Protocol -- U.S. non-party; the UNESCO 2003 Convention for the Safeguarding of the Intangible Cultural Heritage -- U.S. non-party to the Convention, persuasive; UNCLOS -- U.S. non-party, navigational norms accepted as customary international law (Section 16(d)); Mayagna (Sumo) Awas Tingni Community v. Nicaragua (IACtHR, 2001) and Saramaka People v. Suriname (IACtHR, 2007) -- persuasive authority for collective property rights over traditional knowledge and territory; Länsman v. Finland (UN Human Rights Committee, 1994) and Apirana Mahuika v. New Zealand (UN HRC, 2000) -- persuasive. Architectural precedent is additionally drawn from the CARE Principles for Indigenous Data Governance, IEEE 2890-2025, the Local Contexts TK Labels, and the Te Hiku Media Kaitiakitanga License (Aotearoa New Zealand) -- concepts adapted, not merged; Māori and Anishinaabe legal traditions are distinct.

